"""
Local overrides for GOATS.

This file is never touched by GOATS upgrades.
Use it for machine-specific or development-specific customization.

This file is loaded last, after base.py and any environments/<env>.py, so
anything set here wins.

Two things that catch people out
-------------------------------
1. Overriding a value that base.py only used to *build* another setting has no
   effect. For example base.py does::

       DATABASE_TIMEOUT = 20
       DATABASES = {"default": {..., "OPTIONS": {"timeout": DATABASE_TIMEOUT}}}

   By the time this file loads, DATABASES has already been built and holds the
   number 20, not a reference to DATABASE_TIMEOUT. Setting DATABASE_TIMEOUT = 60
   here renames the variable but changes nothing that Django reads. The same
   applies to GOATS_DB_ENGINE and the other GOATS_DB_* values -- they are inputs
   to building DATABASES, not settings in their own right.

   Override the *final* setting instead -- set DATABASES itself (see below), or
   set the GOATS_DB_* environment variables so base.py reads them while it is
   still building.

2. An ImportError anywhere in this file silently discards the whole file. The
   settings loader wraps `from .local import *` in `try/except ImportError` so
   that a missing local.py is not an error -- but that also swallows an
   ImportError raised *by* local.py. If you import something unavailable here,
   every override in this file is skipped with no warning. Keep imports in this
   file to the standard library, or verify with::

       python manage.py shell -c "from django.conf import settings; print(settings.DATABASES)"

Example:
    DEBUG = False
    GPP_ENV = "DEVELOPMENT"

Database
--------
GOATS defaults to SQLite, which is right for a single-user install. Multi-user
deployments -- in particular ones running several ANTARES stream consumers at
once -- should use PostgreSQL, because SQLite permits only one writer at a time.

The usual way to switch is the GOATS_DB_* environment variables (see
GOATS_DB_ENGINE in base.py). Under conda those are most easily made permanent
with an activation script::

    # $CONDA_PREFIX/etc/conda/activate.d/goats_db.sh
    export GOATS_DB_ENGINE=postgres
    export GOATS_DB_NAME=goats
    export GOATS_DB_USER=goats
    export GOATS_DB_PASSWORD=...
    export GOATS_DB_HOST=localhost

If you would rather not depend on the environment, set DATABASES here instead.
This file is never overwritten by GOATS upgrades, and because it loads last it
overrides whatever base.py worked out::

    DATABASES = {
        "default": {
            "ENGINE": "django.db.backends.postgresql",
            "NAME": "goats",
            "USER": "goats",
            "PASSWORD": "...",
            "HOST": "localhost",
            "PORT": "5432",
            "CONN_MAX_AGE": 60,
        }
    }

Either way, PostgreSQL needs a driver that is not installed by default::

    pip install "goats[postgres]"
"""
